    "clauseNumber": "3",
    "title": "Definitions",
    "section": "3 Definitions",
    "page": 16,
    "text": "The following definitions apply in this Standard. Bird holes — holes and damage caused by woodpeckers and other birds. Check — a lengthwise separation of the wood that usually extends across the annual rings, commonly resulting from stresses set up in wood during seasoning. Compression wood — abnormal wood usually formed on the lower side of branches and the concave side of leaning trees. Note: It is characterized by an unusually high proportion of apparent latewood in the annual rings, with a gradual transition from earlywood (springwood) to latewood (summerwood). It has a lifeless appearance and exhibits a darker colour when wetted. Compared to normal wood, compression wood shrinks excessively lengthwise. Crack — Cross break crack — a separation of the wood cells across the grain. Such breaks can occur when a tree is felled, during handling, or be due to internal strains resulting from unequal longitudinal shrinkage. Dead streak — a portion of the sapwood in which the life processes ceased prior to the cutting of the tree. Note: A dead streak differs from a wound such as a scar (cat face) in which the growth of new wood shows that life processes are still acting to repair the injured part. It is characterized by a discoloured, weathered appearance and a lack of growth of healthy wood overlapping the edges of the streak in an attempt to repair the damaged area. Decay (rot, dote, doze) — the deterioration of wood caused by the action of wood-destroying fungi, resulting in softening, loss of strength and weight, and often change of texture and colour. Advanced decay — the late stage of decay in which the decomposition is readily recognized as the wood becomes punky, soft, stringy, pitted, or crumbly. Incipient decay — the early stage of decay in which the decomposition has not proceeded far enough to soften or otherwise change the hardness of the wood perceptibly. It is usually accompanied by a slight discoloration or bleaching of the wood. Note: Laboratory analysis may be required for positive identification of incipient decay. Face of pole — the concave side of a pole having sweep in one plane or the side of greatest curvature between the groundline and the top of a pole having a reverse or double sweep. Groundline section — that section of a pole between 0.305 m (I ft) above and 0.61 m (2 ft) below the groundline as specified in the pole dimension tables of this Standard. Hollow heart — a void in the heartwood caused by decay or insect attack. Insect damage — damage that is the result of boring in the wood by insects or insect larvae (limits as defined in Clause 4-4.6). that portion of a branch or limb that has been surrounded by a subsequent growth of wood of Knot — the trunk or other portion of the tree. Dead knot — a knot resulting from a branch that died before the tree was cut. Decayed knot (unsound knot) — a knot that is softer than the surrounding wood and shows evidence of advanced decay. Knot cluster — two or more knots grouped together as a unit, with the fibres of the wood being deflected around the entire unit; a knot cluster is considered and measured as a single knot. Ring knots — a group of knots that encircle a pole in the same plane; any knot intersecting an imaginary line drawn around a pole is included in the measurement of the sum of diameters of ring knots (see Table 1). Spike knot — an elongated knot that results from a branch that grew on an angle not perpendicular to the trunk of a tree. Note: Spike knots are often encased in outer bark and/or tree sap, frequently resulting in a bark inclusion (limits as defined in Clause 4.4.8.2). Knot diameter — the dimension of a knot measured on the surface of a pole at a right angle to the lengthwise axis of the pole. Note: The sapwood as well as the heartwood portion ofa knot are included in the measurement. Mechanical damage — surface damage incurred during the manufacturing or handling of poles. Plugged holes — holes that have been filled by forcing in a wood insert. Red heart — a form of incipient decay, characterized by a reddish colour produced in the heartwood, that does not render the wood unfit for the majority of uses. Note: Firm red heart contains none of the white pockets that characterize the more advanced stage of decay. It is not to be confused with naturally red heartwood. Sap stain — a discoloration of the sapwood that is caused by the action of certain moulds and fungi and is not accompanied by softening or other deterioration of the wood. Scar (cat face) — a depression in the surface of a pole resulting from a wound where healing has not re- established the normal cross-section of the pole. Shake — separation along the grain, the greater part of which occurs between the annual growth rings. Short crook — a localized deviation from straightness that, within any section 1.52 m (5 ft) or less in length, is more than one-half the mean diameter of the crooked section (see Figure 2). Spiral grain (twisted grain) — a type of growth in which the fibres take a spiral course around the trunk of a tree instead of a vertical course. Note: The spiral can extend around the trunk in a right-hand or left-hand direction or can alternate right and left within the trunk. The degree of spirality in a pole is measured by the distance in feet along the axis of the pole in which one complete twist of the spiral occurs. Split — a separation along the grain forming an opening or fissure that extends through the piece from one surface to another. Sweep — the deviation of a pole from straightness (see Figure I).",
